From 28cb35951c2fd59f99590ac6782f75ce7f8fa594 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 17:01:56 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] cpufreq: Keep Apple Silicon on schedutil in performance

With the performance governor in the performance profile, which
Omarchy selects on AC, a MacBook Pro M4 (J604) holds both clusters at
their top frequency while it idles: 4464 and 2892 MHz, 100 % of the
time, with no transitions. macOS on the same machine idles them at
912 and 1080 MHz on AC.

schedutil on that machine is not the slow ramp that made performance
worth it elsewhere: a one-thread burst on an efficiency core reaches
its top state in a median of 18 ms (macOS ramps its P cluster in 71
to 75 ms; the P cluster's ramp under schedutil is not measured). It
also keeps the scheduler's energy-aware placement, which needs
schedutil, so idle work stays on the efficiency cores as on macOS.

Keep apple-cpufreq on schedutil in every profile, and say so in the
README and the driver's description. The boost switch and the
power-saver caps are unchanged.

Test: meson test, 187 OK, 8 skipped, 0 failed.
---
 README.md                 | 13 +++++++------
 src/ppd-driver-cpufreq.c  | 24 +++++++++++++++++-------
 tests/integration_test.py | 18 ++++++++++--------
 3 files changed, 34 insertions(+), 21 deletions(-)

diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
index 6662eb0..5ca5745 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -256,9 +256,10 @@ today.
 
 The "power-saver" profile turns boost off, which keeps the CPU at or below its
 highest sustainable frequency. The "balanced" and "performance" profiles turn
-it on. The scaling governor follows the profile: `performance` in
-"performance", and `schedutil` otherwise, set back to `schedutil` when the
-daemon stops. The boost switch has no setting that
+it on. The scaling governor stays `schedutil` in every profile, as macOS
+keeps its clusters at low clocks at idle: the `performance` governor held a
+MacBook Pro M4's clusters at their top frequency at idle on AC. It is set
+back to `schedutil` when the daemon stops. The boost switch has no setting that
 depends on the power source, so it follows the selected profile on battery and
 on AC alike.
 
@@ -273,9 +274,9 @@ The other clusters stay uncapped. "balanced" and "performance" are
 uncapped, the same split as with the boost switch. The boost switch is left
 alone, so it need not be writable, but every policy needs a
 `scaling_available_frequencies` table, as with the caps below. The scaling
-governor follows the profile as with the boost switch: on a MacBook Neo (A18
-Pro), Speedometer 3.1 scored 28.8 under `schedutil` and 34.4 under
-`performance`.
+governor stays `schedutil` in every profile, as with the boost switch. The
+trade-off: on a MacBook Neo (A18 Pro), Speedometer 3.1 scored 28.8 under
+`schedutil` and 34.4 under `performance`.
 
 On machines whose CPU frequency is managed by Arm SCMI or Qualcomm firmware,
 such as the Qualcomm Snapdragon X series, the same driver caps each policy's
diff --git a/src/ppd-driver-cpufreq.c b/src/ppd-driver-cpufreq.c
index 1d4ceb1..d015fa5 100644
--- a/src/ppd-driver-cpufreq.c
+++ b/src/ppd-driver-cpufreq.c
@@ -16,7 +16,8 @@
  * - boost: the global boost switch, see "Frequency Boost Support" in
  *   https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/pm/cpufreq.html
  *   Used where turning boost off is known to cap the CPU at its sustainable
- *   frequency. The governor follows the profile as with the caps below.
+ *   frequency. Its only user, apple-cpufreq, stays on schedutil in every
+ *   profile, see below.
  *
  * - caps: each policy's scaling_max_freq, capped in power-saver at a share
  *   of the policy's highest non-boost frequency, the way Windows' processor
@@ -45,11 +46,14 @@
  * none, so it uses the caps method instead. Only power-saver is capped there, which
  * keeps the boost method's split: balanced and performance run uncapped. As
  * macOS's Low Power mode does, power-saver caps only the fastest cluster
- * there, at 47%, and leaves the efficiency cluster uncapped. Its
- * governor follows the profile as with any caps: on a MacBook Neo (A18 Pro),
- * Speedometer 3.1 scored 28.8 under schedutil and 34.4 under performance,
- * so the performance governor stays in performance there, as with the boost
- * method.
+ * there, at 47%, and leaves the efficiency cluster uncapped.
+ *
+ * Every apple-cpufreq machine, with either method, stays on schedutil in
+ * every profile. The performance governor held a MacBook Pro M4's clusters
+ * at their top frequency at idle on AC, where macOS idles them at 912 and
+ * 1080 MHz; schedutil takes an efficiency core to its top in 18 ms there.
+ * The trade-off: on a MacBook Neo (A18 Pro), Speedometer 3.1 scored 28.8
+ * under schedutil and 34.4 under performance.
  *
  * It is only used when every cpufreq policy runs one of the scaling drivers
  * in the table below. Any other system keeps its current driver.
@@ -443,7 +447,13 @@ ppd_driver_cpufreq_probe (PpdDriver *driver)
   case CPUFREQ_METHOD_NONE:
     return PPD_PROBE_RESULT_FAIL;
   case CPUFREQ_METHOD_BOOST:
-    cpufreq->performance_governor = TRUE;
+    /* Apple Silicon stays on schedutil in performance too. With the
+     * performance governor a MacBook Pro M4 (J604) on AC holds both clusters
+     * at their top frequency while idle (P 4464, E 2892 MHz, 100 %), where
+     * macOS idles them at 912 and 1080 MHz, and schedutil there reaches the
+     * top in 18 ms (macOS ramps its P cluster in 71 to 75 ms). It also keeps
+     * the scheduler's energy-aware placement, which needs schedutil. */
+    cpufreq->performance_governor = FALSE;
     if (boost_can_be_enabled (policy_dirs)) {
       ret = probe_boost (cpufreq);
       break;
diff --git a/tests/integration_test.py b/tests/integration_test.py
